{"format":"kards-original-reference","version":1,"id":"203ed046701a3fb6","name":"card_unit_duck_us","package":"/Game/Blueprints/Cards/USA/OceaniaStorm/units/card_unit_duck_us","parent":"UBaseCardObject","card":{"title":"DUCK","text":"Gets +1+1 and you Forecast when you move it into the frontline.","flavorText":"The Grumman J2F Duck was a single-engine amphibious biplane used by the US Navy, Marine Corps, Coast Guard, and Army Air Forces. It first flew in 1936 and was an improved version of the earlier JF Duck, with a longer central float and a stronger engine. A total of 584 J2F Ducks were built, including 330 produced by Columbia Aircraft after US entry into WWII increased demand. The Duck looked old-fashioned beside faster wartime aircraft, but its value came from versatility. Its large central float also held a retractable landing gear, allowing it to operate from both water and land. Crews used it for utility flights, light transport, scouting, mapping, photographic surveys, anti-submarine patrols, target towing, and air-sea rescue. It could carry two passengers or a stretcher inside the fuselage. One Duck made a notable escape from Bataan in 1942. After repairs using parts from another damaged aircraft, it departed after midnight on 9 April, carrying five passengers and pilot Roland J. Barnick. It was the last aircraft to leave Bataan before the Allied surrender there. Ducks later served at Guadalcanal in search and rescue missions.","faction":"USA","type":"fighter","rarity":"Common","cost":1,"operationCost":1,"attack":1,"defense":2},"defaults":{"UberGraphFrame":{},"GameplayTags":["ability.forecast"],"Name":"card_unit_duck_us","title":{"Namespace":"card_usa","Key":"card_unit_duck_us_title","SourceString":"DUCK","LocalizedString":"DUCK"},"Type":"ETypeEnum::fighter","attack":1,"defense":2,"range":2,"faction":"EFactionEnum::USA","kredits":1,"Text":{"Namespace":"card_usa","Key":"card_unit_duck_us_text","SourceString":"Gets +1+1 and you Forecast when you move it into the frontline.","LocalizedString":"Gets +1+1 and you Forecast when you move it into the frontline."},"flavorText":{"Namespace":"card_usa","Key":"card_unit_duck_us_history","SourceString":"The Grumman J2F Duck was a single-engine amphibious biplane used by the US Navy, Marine Corps, Coast Guard, and Army Air Forces. It first flew in 1936 and was an improved version of the earlier JF Duck, with a longer central float and a stronger engine. A total of 584 J2F Ducks were built, including 330 produced by Columbia Aircraft after US entry into WWII increased demand. The Duck looked old-fashioned beside faster wartime aircraft, but its value came from versatility. Its large central float also held a retractable landing gear, allowing it to operate from both water and land. Crews used it for utility flights, light transport, scouting, mapping, photographic surveys, anti-submarine patrols, target towing, and air-sea rescue. It could carry two passengers or a stretcher inside the fuselage. One Duck made a notable escape from Bataan in 1942. After repairs using parts from another damaged aircraft, it departed after midnight on 9 April, carrying five passengers and pilot Roland J. Barnick. It was the last aircraft to leave Bataan before the Allied surrender there. Ducks later served at Guadalcanal in search and rescue missions.","LocalizedString":"The Grumman J2F Duck was a single-engine amphibious biplane used by the US Navy, Marine Corps, Coast Guard, and Army Air Forces. It first flew in 1936 and was an improved version of the earlier JF Duck, with a longer central float and a stronger engine. A total of 584 J2F Ducks were built, including 330 produced by Columbia Aircraft after US entry into WWII increased demand. The Duck looked old-fashioned beside faster wartime aircraft, but its value came from versatility. Its large central float also held a retractable landing gear, allowing it to operate from both water and land. Crews used it for utility flights, light transport, scouting, mapping, photographic surveys, anti-submarine patrols, target towing, and air-sea rescue. It could carry two passengers or a stretcher inside the fuselage. One Duck made a notable escape from Bataan in 1942. After repairs using parts from another damaged aircraft, it departed after midnight on 9 April, carrying five passengers and pilot Roland J. Barnick. It was the last aircraft to leave Bataan before the Allied surrender there. Ducks later served at Guadalcanal in search and rescue missions."},"operationCost":1,"rarity":"ERarityEnum::Common","cardSet":"ECardSetEnum::OceaniaStorm","EffectType":"ECardEffectTypes::Trigger","usedTriggers":["ERegisteredCardFunction::OnStartofTurn"],"spawnCardName":"card_event_sunny1_blue_sky;card_event_rain1_mist;card_event_storm1_gale","historyPhoto":{"AssetPathName":"/Game/Assets/Textures/HistoricalPhotos/DUCK.DUCK","SubPathString":""},"historyPhotoDescription":{"Namespace":"card_usa","Key":"card_unit_duck_us_photo","SourceString":"A US Navy Grumman J2F-5 Duck on a anti-submarine patrol, 1942. The aircraft is armed with Mark XVII depth bombs.","LocalizedString":"A US Navy Grumman J2F-5 Duck on a anti-submarine patrol, 1942. The aircraft is armed with Mark XVII depth bombs."},"blowUpExplosion":{"ObjectName":"ParticleSystem'P_ExplosionAircraftS'","ObjectPath":"kards/Content/Effects/Explosions/Small/Aircraft/P_ExplosionAircraftS.119"},"blowUpCameraShake":{"ObjectName":"BlueprintGeneratedClass'CameraShakeLight_C'","ObjectPath":"kards/Content/examples/GrenadePack/Blueprints/CameraShakeLight.0"},"OnAttackVisualsSoft":{"AssetPathName":"/Game/Blueprints/Effects/BP_AttackFighter.BP_AttackFighter_C","SubPathString":""}},"variables":[],"functions":[{"name":"ExecuteUbergraph_card_unit_duck_us","owner":"card_unit_duck_us_C","params":[{"name":"EntryPoint","type":"int32","direction":"in"}],"pure":false,"event":false,"flags":"FUNC_Final | FUNC_UbergraphFunction","locals":[{"name":"K2Node_Event_ForceMove","type":"bool","direction":"in"},{"na